{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Current efforts in improving the recovery of central nervous system (CNS) focus on two aspects: 1) stimulation of neurogenesis or regeneration and 2) neuroprotection or prevention of secondary damage (4, 5). However, there is still significant disagreement over the new treatment strategies. For instance, older studies had demonstrated that inflammation caused by autoimmune response leads to exacerbation of SCI and motor impairment (6, 7). Nonetheless, recent animal research showed that the postinjury autoimmune reactions provoke beneficial endogenous responses following SCI (8).",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge regarding SCI treatment strategies, specifically the conflicting views on autoimmune responses, and highlights the uncertainties surrounding the effectiveness of immunization."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Although several studies that evaluate the effectiveness of active and passive immunization in SCI are available, conflicting findings have been reported between studies... These inconsistencies have made it impossible to draw a general conclusion in this regard. On the other hand, it is not yet clear which active and inactive immunogenic methods are more effective in improving the motor function of animals. Accordingly, there is still no conclusion on the role of passive immunization with immune cells activated by MBP or on active immunization with injection of MBP and its derivatives in the treatment of SCI in the literature. Hence, the present study aims to perform a systematic review and meta-analysis to determine the effectiveness of passive or active immunization with MBP on motor recovery in animal models following SCI.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the need for the review by citing conflicting findings in existing literature and the lack of a general conclusion regarding the effectiveness of active and passive immunization with MBP."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The manuscript does not mention the existence of previous systematic reviews on this specific topic, so this conditional requirement does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "These studies indicate that the presence of immune T cells in the injury site increases secretion of nervous growth factors, improves the tissue environment surrounding the damaged neurons, protects the remaining myelin, and eventually, leads to enhancement of motor recovery (4).",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the proposed mechanism of action for the immunization intervention (immune T cells increasing growth factors and protecting myelin)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not involve complex interventions or delivery contexts that require a logic model."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "PICO was defined as follows: Problem (P) included animals in which SCI injury was induced. Intervention (I) was the injection of MBP-activated T cells (passive immunization) or administration of MBP or MBP-modified peptides, inducing intrinsic immunity to MBP (active immunization). Comparison (C) consisted of a comparison with control animals, which were induced with SCI but had not received treatment, and outcome (O) was an assessment of motor function by BBB testing.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly states its objective and research question using the PICO framework."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "PICO was defined as follows: Problem (P) included animals in which SCI injury was induced. Intervention (I) was the injection of MBP-activated T cells (passive immunization) or administration of MBP or MBP-modified peptides, inducing intrinsic immunity to MBP (active immunization). Comparison (C) consisted of a comparison with control animals, which were induced with SCI but had not received treatment, and outcome (O) was an assessment of motor function by BBB testing.",
      "reason": "The planned comparisons (Intervention vs Control) are explicitly stated using the PICO framework."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria in the present study consisted of controlled studies that had evaluated the efficacy of immune cells activated against MBP protein in motor function improvementafter SCI. Controlled studies were defined as studies that had a control group without any treatment (placebo group or vehicle group), in addition to the group treated with active/passive immunization with MBP . ... Moreover, lack of motor function evaluation and combination therapy were the other exclusion criteria in the current meta-analysis.",
      "reason": "The authors specify the study design (controlled studies), the population (SCI), the intervention (active/passive immunization with MBP), and the outcome (motor function improvement). Additionally, chunk_id text_6 specifies a follow-up requirement: \"studies with a follow-up period of less than four weeks were excluded.\""
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Since some articles were written in Chinese and Japanese, no language restrictions were imposed.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state that no language restrictions were imposed. While dissemination year is not explicitly mentioned as a criterion, the search strategy (text_4) notes the search was conducted \"until the end of 2020,\" which serves as a temporal restriction."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The manuscript does not explicitly distinguish between exclusion due to outcomes not being measured versus exclusion due to outcome results not being reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "If the studies were homogeneous, the fixed effect model was performed, and in case of heterogeneity, subgroup analysis was conducted to determine the source of the heterogeneity. ... Meta-analyses were performed, only if the data were reported in at least two separate experiments.",
      "reason": "The authors specify the grouping strategy for synthesis, noting that meta-analyses were performed if data were available in at least two experiments, and that subgroup analysis was used to address heterogeneity."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "Considering that motor function recovery following SCI needs a 4-week time window after the injury in a rat model, studies with a follow-up period of less than four weeks were excluded.",
      "reason": "The authors provide a biological rationale for the restriction on the follow-up period (4 weeks)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 95
}

